# Authoring a skill

Follow the Agent Skills spec (https://agentskills.io/specification).

## Frontmatter

```yaml
---
name: kebab-case-matching-the-directory   # required, max 64 chars, a-z 0-9 and hyphens
description: >                             # required, max 1024 chars
  What it does, then when to apply it. Name the concrete triggers: the packages
  involved, the file names, and the phrases a user actually types.
license: MIT
metadata:
  author: fodilfliti
  version: "0.1.0"
  repo: https://github.com/fodilfliti/skills
---
```

`description` is loaded at startup for every installed skill, so it is an activation trigger, not a summary. Write "Apply when the user says X / when file Y exists / when package Z is in pubspec", not "This skill covers X".

## Body

- Under ~500 lines. If it grows past that, move detail into a sibling file and link it from a short "read only when needed" list at the top.
- Progressive disclosure: name and description at startup, body on activation, sibling files on demand.
- Lead with the check that gates everything else — for this family that is "read `lemsa.yaml` first".
- Prefer a table of rules and short code snippets over prose.
- End with a `## Do / don't` section. Concrete, not aspirational.

## Self-containment

`npx skills add` copies a single skill directory. So:

- No links to `spec/`, to the repo root, or to another skill's files.
- Cross-skill dependencies are prose: "if `pubspec.yaml` lists `supabase_flutter`, also load the `flutter-supabase` skill".
- Repeating a rule across two skills is acceptable when both need it standalone. Repeating rationale is not — rationale belongs in `spec/`.

## When a public API changes

Update the skill that teaches it in the same change. A skill that documents an API that no longer exists is worse than no skill.
